## Why 0.5 and -0.5? Understanding Wheel Placement

Here's something that confused me at first: why are the wheels positioned at 0.5 and -0.5? This matters more than it sounds.

Think of the robot from above. The two wheels are separated by a distance—0.5 meters to one side, 0.5 meters to the other side. This distance is called the wheelbase. If the wheels are far apart, the robot makes wide, gentle turns. If they're close together, the robot spins tighter. Real robots tune this distance based on what they need to do.

The 0.5 (left wheel) and -0.5 (right wheel) are on the Y-axis, which means they're offset sideways. Both wheels are aligned front-to-back, so the robot balances naturally. If one wheel was forward and one was back, the robot would be lopsided.

The wheels spin around the Y-axis—imagine an axis running left-to-right through the robot. That's the rotation axis for rolling wheels. If the axis ran up-down instead, the wheels would flip like a steering wheel on a car, not roll like ground wheels. If it ran front-to-back, the wheels would fly off sideways.

The wheels are defined as "continuous" joints, which means they can spin forever. If they were "limited" joints, they'd stop at some maximum rotation angle. Real wheels don't stop spinning—they roll indefinitely. So "continuous" is the only option that makes sense.

## How Import Works

When I imported the URDF file, Isaac Sim converted it into USD format (Unisonverse Scene Description—the format Isaac Sim uses internally). Each part of the robot became a 3D object (called a prim). Each connection became a joint. The physics engine read all the masses and geometries and set up the physics calculations.

Since the URDF didn't specify how "stiff" the wheel joints should be (the resistance to bending), Isaac Sim calculated default values based on the wheel shape and mass. This works okay for simulation but might not perfectly match a real robot's wheel.

Once I enabled PhysX physics on the robot, the engine started calculating realistic motion. Everything became subject to gravity and collision physics.

